comments.body:
At this point:
```
    30          if ((setup_res = setup_tests()) > 0) {
    31              ret = run_tests(argv[0]);
*   32              cleanup_tests();
    33              opt_check_usage();
```
`ret` is -1, which causes the test runner to report a failure. This is not a SIGSEGV. From what I can tell, there are two failures (`num_failed` == 2), both are related to being unable to load a DLL. I am recompiling with `--debug` to see which one.

@reporter right, I get what you are saying, but in your previous [comment ](https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/22819#issuecomment-1826439971), you had noted that dasync.so wasn't present in the either the global location, or in the build itself, so I wanted to confirm that it was actually built.

As for why the global dir is being specified during your test, I'm unsure.  Looking at run_tests.pl, OPENSSL_ENGINES should be set to $bldtop/engines, so /path/to/your/build/engines should be whats specified during the test run.  I'm at something of a loss for why/how that would be overridden.

It might be worthwhile to augment test/recipies/30_test_evp_extra.t to print out the value of OPENSSL_ENGINES to see what its pointing to, just to confirm that the path is whats expected.  That would inform us as to weather or not the variable is getting overridden in the test harness, or somewhere in the C code itself.

A few notes based on your comments here:
https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/22819#issuecomment-1828468718
1) Calling OPENSSL_init_crypto multiple times should be ok (or at least its supposed to be).  The intent is that multiple calls to OPENSSL_init_crypto should initalize the library on the first call, and subsequent calls should effectively be a no-op.
2) I notice something odd about the backtrace in your comment:
```
Breakpoint 1, OPENSSL_init_crypto (opts=134851648, settings=0x100000045)
    at /home/<user>/.jenkins/workspace/OpenSSL-3.0_Monitor/crypto/init.c:463
```
In the second call to OPENSSL_init_crypto, the options field shows a value of 134851648 (hex 809AC40).  Thats odd because this call is made from ossl_engine_table_select like this:
```
ENGINE *ossl_engine_table_select(ENGINE_TABLE **table, int nid,
                                 const char *f, int l)
{
    ENGINE *ret = NULL;
    ENGINE_PILE tmplate, *fnd = NULL;
    int initres, loop = 0;

    /* Load the config before trying to check if engines are available */
    OPENSSL_init_crypto(OPENSSL_INIT_LOAD_CONFIG, NULL);
```
OPENSSL_INIT_LOAD_CONFIG is defined as 0x00000040

So I'm a bit concerned that somehow the opts value on the stack is somehow getting corrupted (or the debugger is showing us incorrect information).  Not sure of the relevance here, but perhaps if the options are getting corrupted, we may be following an incorrect path through OPENSSL_init_crypto, which may cause the problems being observed.

Question: Is there a libasan library available for nonstop?  Something to validate that we're not getting data corruptors in the nonstop build that aren't present on other platforms?
